@page "/learn/renderfragment-across-render-modes"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Static content in interactive components · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Static content in interactive components</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You've built an interactive card. Why does a static page fail when it passes a table in as <code>ChildContent</code>?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Rendering · Static SSR with an Interactive Server card · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a static page can pass <code>ChildContent</code>, or any plain
        <code>RenderFragment</code>, to an interactive component. In .NET 10 the same code compiled cleanly, then
        failed every request with a 500: <i>"RenderFragment, which is arbitrary code and cannot be serialized"</i>.
        There's no new API: .NET 11 fixed it inside the framework.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Warehouse</i> page. It's static: the server renders it once, and no circuit is needed. It queries
        the products that are low on stock, and puts the table inside <code>Collapsible</code>, a small card that runs
        on Interactive Server so its header can fold it away.
    </p>
    <p>
        Folding on its own doesn't need a circuit: a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> element does it in plain HTML. The
        card stands in for the wrappers that do need C#: tabs that load on demand, a dialog with its own state, a panel
        that remembers what the user chose. What matters is the shape: an interactive component, static markup inside.
    </p>
    <p>
        Because .NET 11 fixed this in the framework, the old failure can't run here. So Fig. 1 replays what our .NET 10
        build returned for the same page, unchanged.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The page, as .NET 10 ran it" Url="demo/static-content/low-stock/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            A recording, not a live page, so its buttons don't change anything. The address bar shows this demo's own
            address; the request it replays is <code>GET /warehouse</code>, our .NET 10 copy of the page in Fig. 3.
            That build had no warnings.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>.NET 10 won't send code to an interactive component</h2>
    <p>The table between the card's tags isn't data. It's a <code>RenderFragment</code>, a delegate:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What the static page tries to pass to the card</span>
        <pre class="snip">Collapsible  <span class="dim">@@rendermode="InteractiveServer"</span>
├─ Title         <span class="dim">string</span>          <span class="ok">✓ serialized, sent to the circuit</span>
└─ ChildContent  <span class="dim">RenderFragment</span>  <span class="err">a delegate: arbitrary code</span>
   └─ &lt;p&gt; Queried at, &lt;table&gt; ...</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        An interactive component starts later, on the circuit, so its parameters have to travel there as data. A
        string can. A delegate can't. .NET 10 checks this while the static page renders, and throws
        <code>InvalidOperationException</code>: <i>"which is arbitrary code and cannot be serialized"</i>. The build
        gives no warning.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>None in your code</h2>
    <p>The page that failed on .NET 10 is the fix. Pass markup to an interactive component as usual:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole page, in short</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;Collapsible @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" Title="Low stock"&gt;</span>
    &lt;p&gt;Queried at @@queriedAt&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;table&gt;...&lt;/table&gt;
<span class="hl">&lt;/Collapsible&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        .NET 11 doesn't send the code. The static page runs the fragment while it prerenders, records the markup it
        produced, and sends that to the card. On the circuit, the card replays it like any other
        <code>ChildContent</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What doesn't cross, silently</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;Collapsible @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" Title="Low stock"&gt;
    &lt;button <span class="err">@@onclick="Reorder"</span>&gt;Reorder&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/Collapsible&gt;</pre>
        <div class="verdict bad">✗ the button renders, but its handler is dropped, with only a warning in the server log. The same goes for <code>@@ref</code> and <code>@@formname</code>. A button that should do something in C# belongs in the interactive component, not in the markup you pass it.</div>
    </div>
    <p>Here's the same page, live on .NET 11.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same page, on .NET 11" Url="demo/static-content/low-stock"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            Wait for <b>Interactive</b> in the card's corner. Click <b>▾ Low stock</b> to fold the card, and again to
            open it: the click runs in C# on the server. The table comes back with the same <b>Queried at</b> time,
            because the page's query didn't run again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page, then the card:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/StaticContent/LowStock.razor" Feature="renderfragment-across-render-modes">
        <Caption>
            The page both figures run. Fig. 1 is this file on .NET 10; Fig. 2 is this file on .NET 11, unchanged.
            The highlighted lines aren't a change: they mark the fragment that crosses the boundary.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/StaticContent/Collapsible.razor" Feature="renderfragment-across-render-modes">
        <Caption>
            The card, also unchanged. The highlights mark the <code>ChildContent</code> parameter and where it
            renders. It starts open, because the page only records the fragment if it renders on the first pass.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Move the boundary, or move the table</h2>
    <p>With no way to pass the table across, you moved one or the other:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways round it in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/warehouse"
<span class="err">@@rendermode InteractiveServer</span></pre>
                <span class="why">no boundary to cross, so the card folds and reopens</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every visitor holds a circuit for the whole page, and the fragment's code re-runs there: a stamp in it changes on every re-render</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;LowStockCard @@rendermode="InteractiveServer"
              <span class="err">Products="products"</span> /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">the table moves into the interactive component, which gets the data instead</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a new card for every table, each with its own copy of the folding logic</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Either way, one reusable interactive wrapper around static content from many pages wasn't possible: the
        content had to become interactive too, or move inside the wrapper.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>What crosses is a snapshot</h2>
    <p>
        The first workaround re-runs the fragment's code on the circuit, so a stamp in it changes on every re-render.
        In .NET 11 the fragment's code runs once, in the static render, and the card only ever replays that output.
        That's why <b>Queried at</b> in Fig. 2 never moves. It also means the content can't update: anything that
        should change while the user watches has to load inside the card.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Render it on the first pass.</b> With <code>prerender: false</code>, or content hidden behind an <code>@@if</code> until later, there's nothing to record, and it throws when the page runs. The build can't catch it. That's why the card starts open.</li>
        <li><b>Only plain <code>RenderFragment</code> crosses.</b> A <code>RenderFragment&lt;T&gt;</code>, such as a grid's row template, still throws as it did in .NET 10.</li>
        <li><b>Mind the size.</b> The recorded markup travels in the component's marker, several times bigger than the HTML, and SignalR's default limit is 32 KB per message. The dashboard's two tables went past it, so <code>Program.cs</code> raises <code>MaximumReceiveMessageSize</code> to 64 KB. A short table is fine; a long report isn't.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
